the wandb runs were backfilled from `runs/*/metrics.jsonl` and the eval records after the fact (the trainer writes jsonl; wandb mirrors and never decides), so they hold everything the trainer logged: losses, entropy, KL, clip fraction, game length, seat win rates, the critic's calibration (`critic/value_sum_*`: the per-seat win probabilities should sum to about the share of games somebody wins), throughput and the select curve. `ppo-4p`'s critic summed to about 1.2 over its four seats against a realized 0.88, a bias from V-trace targets on waiting rows; `configs/experiments/ppo-4p-critic-fix.yaml` fixed it on 2026-10-18 (below). since 2026-10-17 there is a live mirror (`catanrl.wandb_mirror`, on whenever an experiment's `meta.wandb.project` is set; the record on disk stays the source of truth and every wandb failure is a warning) and `tools/wandb_backfill.py` re-syncs any run directory into the same wandb run, so a run launched without the mirror, like `ppo-mixed`, lands there too.

== what is still missing for this section

- #done *the ~5% search edge in v2, head to head.* v1 measured MCTS against the same network's belief-averaged raw policy on 1,000 games: 51.1% at 256 sims, 55.1% at 1024, 58.1% at 4096 (against F both are near the ceiling, which is why the vs-F rows above don't move). v2, same network, fair search (`dets=1`, worlds from the hand tracker) against `BELIEF` 16 worlds on `heldout-2p`, finished 2026-10-18: *53.4* #ci[50.3][56.5] at 1024 sims (n=1,000, `04fd2c25`) and *55.2* #ci[50.8][59.5] at 4096 (n=500, `2aaf2eb7`). so the edge is there and grows with sims, a point or two smaller than v1's over a stronger raw policy; both seats of the pairing agree (first seat 55.0 / second 51.8 at 1024). cost: 11.5 h of gpu for the 1,500 games at 128 games in flight, which is what parks alphazero at these sim counts (@sec:ideas). the `public` network's own rows (next item) repeat it over its raw policy: 54.9 at 1024, 55.2 at 4096, in 5.0 h.
- #done *the fair 2p variant.* `puffer-graph2/public` (the `public` level: in 2p every resource movement is seen by both seats, so the opponent's exact hand is a perfect card counter's knowledge, and a `public` policy is fair without belief averaging; what `full` adds over it is only the opponent's dev cards in hand) ran 2026-10-18 18:03 to 2026-10-19 02:06: the same recipe and budget as `full`, the same 7.95 h at 17.8k steps/s, best checkpoint e3600 (select 96.5 against `full`'s e3350 at 96.8; the two select curves lie on top of each other, @fig:curves, mean over epochs ≥ 3000 94.4 vs 95.1). final: *94.96* #ci[94.3][95.5] vs F on `heldout-2p` as a plain fair `POLICY` (`dc8a611a`, 5,000 games), against the `full` variant's belief-averaged 95.06 #ci[94.4][95.6] and its oracle 95.16, so the one thing `public` cannot see, the opponent's dev cards, is worth nothing measurable in 2p (the aux head found the same from the other side: the hand is inferable, the dev cards are not, and the policy did not need them). one forward pass, no sampled worlds, no tracker: this is the bot to serve raw. its own search rows ran 02:07 to 07:10 (5.0 h for the 1,500 games, half of what the `full` finals took: the opponent here is one forward pass, not sixteen, and 256 games were in flight instead of 128): MCTS 1024 sims, one world from the tracker, beats the raw `public` policy *54.9* #ci[51.8][58.0] on 1,000 games (`72819d1a`, 1.5 h) and 4096 sims *55.2* #ci[50.8][59.5] on 500 (`0ef1b81e`, 3.6 h), the same edge the `full` network has over its belief-averaged self (53.4 / 55.2 above). both seats agree (the searcher 60.6 in seat 0 and 49.2 in seat 1 at 1024, the raw policy 50.8 / 39.4), with a first-seat advantage under both: seat 0 wins about 56% of these games whoever sits there.
- #done *the critic fix.* `ppo-4p`'s critic summed its four seats' win probabilities above the realized sum (about 1.2 against 0.87 at epoch 300; 0.93 against 0.90 by the end), from two causes found on cpu replications: V-trace ratios on the waiting rows of each sample (only the acting seat's row gets min(ρ, 1), the other three get 1, so each seat's target values the game as if that seat alone played the improved policy, and the four one-sided gains compound through bootstrapping) and muon's fixed-size update of the 1 × 128 value output layer (the rollout network lands on the high side every epoch). `configs/experiments/ppo-4p-critic-fix.yaml`, run 2026-10-18 (four arms × 15 min): 100-epoch fine-tunes of `ppo-4p/main@latest` at its final learning rate with lambda-return critic targets (every ratio 1; the policy's advantages stay V-trace's), at three discounts and one with the output layer on AdamW. every arm puts the seat sum on the realized sum within about 20 epochs and keeps it there (@fig:critic): `lambda` 0.906 against 0.899 realized at γ = 0.999 (from 0.920 against 0.914 in the first epochs), `lambda-critic-g1` 0.998 against 1.000 with the critic undiscounted, `lambda-g1` 1.000 against 1.000 with policy and critic undiscounted, `lambda-adamw` 0.906 against 0.898. the rollout-vs-trained gap falls from 0.004 to 0.000 in all four, so the targets alone remove muon's phase lock and the AdamW arm buys nothing. per-slot calibration at the end of `lambda-critic-g1`: the seat to move predicted 0.284 against a realized 0.276, the other three within 0.004. the policy does not move in 100 epochs: select-4p 79.9 at the start and 80.4 / 79.9 / 80.4 / 79.1 at the end of the four arms (n=800 per point, ±2.8), clip fraction, entropy and game length (79–80 turns) unchanged, and the undiscounted policy plays games of the same length, so γ = 1 changes nothing the policy optimises at this horizon. recommendation, applied to the defaults on 2026-10-19: `critic_targets: lambda` and `critic_gamma: 1` in `configs/train/ppo.yaml` for every run from now on (per-seat win probabilities that sum to one are what max^n backups want); the experiment yamls with stored runs pin the previous values, so their config hashes and their records are unchanged. from scratch (2026-10-19, the two `aux-hidden-4p` arms, the first runs under the new default; @fig:critic, right): the undiscounted lambda critic overshoots early, a seat sum of 1.83 at epoch 21, 1.39 at 50, 1.31 at 100, 1.18 at 200, then lands on the realized 1.00 by epoch 220, where the learning rate is still four fifths of its peak, and holds a mean of 1.000 over epochs 300–750, within 0.06 at every epoch (the aux arm the same, mean 0.99). `ppo-4p/main`'s V-trace critic on the same recipe read 1.27 at epoch 100, 1.21 at 300, 1.09 at 500, 1.06 at 750 and never got there (0.94 at epoch 3800 against a realized 0.90). so the early overshoot is a from-scratch transient the fine-tunes could not show (their critics started trained) and both target rules produce it; only the lambda critic recovers from it. explained variance 0.82–0.85 at the end, as before. live logs: the `ppo-4p-critic-fix` group on wandb.
- #done *the 4p search rows.* `configs/experiments/search-4p.yaml`, run 2026-10-18 (71 min for 400 games): `MCTS 1024 dets=1` with the critic-fix network (`ppo-4p-critic-fix/lambda-critic-g1@e100`, undiscounted per-seat values that sum to one, the same raw policy as `ppo-4p` to within noise) against three copies of that network's raw policy on `heldout-4p`, 100 boards × 4 rotations: a 27.0 #ci[22.9][31.6] share (`90a1f06c`) against the raw copies' 24.3 and a parity of 25. so the search edge in 4p is about two points of share (8% relative), the same order as the 2p edge's 3.4 points of win rate, and 400 games cannot separate it from zero. `configs/experiments/search-4p-2000.yaml`, the same table on 500 boards × 4 rotations, ran 2026-10-19 07:38 to 10:15 (2.6 h for 2,000 games with 512 in flight, 4.7 s a game; the first 100 boards replay the record above, 400 of its 400 games identical, and the other 1,600 alone give 26.9): *26.9* #ci[25.0][28.9] (`657b314d`, 538 wins of 2,000) against the raw copies' 25.2 / 22.7 / 25.3 (24.4 pooled). the point estimate did not move and the interval now ends on parity (p = 0.053 against 25% by the record's own test), and the searcher is ahead of the raw copies' mean at every seat (+3.4 in seat 0, +2.1, +3.3 and +1.3 in seats 1–3). read it as a two-point edge, 8% relative, measured to about ±2: the same relative edge as 2p's (7–10% there), real but small at 1024 sims and one world, and a long way from paying for its cost (each searcher decision is 1024 forwards). the first seat wins 27.9% of these games overall against seat 2's 23.1, so a small first-seat advantage sits in these numbers too (no F at the table, so no F targeting; the four rotations are summed).
- #done *tree reuse and leaf deduplication in the search* (marco's ask, 2026-10-20, for the deployed bot). the engine's pool could re-root a search at a child (`advance`) but the agent never asked, and chance outcomes of one node were merged while positions reached by different action orders were not. built in a worktree the same night: `MCTS:...:reuse=true` keeps a seat's search alive across its decisions and re-roots it when the game advanced by exactly its own action (a fresh simulation budget on top of the retained visits; about a third of a seat's decisions in 4p and half in 2p re-root, the rest follow a roll, another seat's move or a forced move); `transpositions=true` gives the pool a per-world transposition table (positions reached by different build, buy or discard orders share one node with shared statistics; the discard chain after a 7 collapses from a tree to a lattice of at most fifty nodes against 86; links per search from a handful to over a hundred by position; the simulation count is identical and the search deeper rather than smaller); and the search driver evaluates byte-identical rows once (bitwise neutral under the strict evaluator, so it has no key; rows forwarded to the network fall to 46–91% depending on worlds and players, by a cache of rows seen in earlier rounds). both options are keyed only when set, so every stored search record rebuilds unchanged. the comparison rows against the stored search tables (`mcts-reuse-2p`, `mcts-reuse-4p-both`, `mcts-reuse-4p`, paired on shared game ids with `tools/search_probe.py --pair`) ran after the trading rows. 2p (`mcts-reuse-2p`, 04:24–05:09, 45 min for 200 games): the searcher with both options takes *58.5* #ci[51.6][65.1] of the games against the stored row's 59.5 on the same 200 game ids (`12f704f2` against `72819d1a`), paired −1.0 pp with 7 games flipping one way and 9 the other (p = 0.80): no regression, and mostly the same games, which is what a deeper search at the same simulation count looks like at this budget; the strength question is the follow-up row, equal share at half the sims. reuse on the server bridge, for the deployed bots, is merged (a seat's search survives its own moves; one fresh search in a recorded 1v1 took 40 s on a busy box against a 2 s median, under investigation); the openhex seat is being wired to it.

