= exploring ideas <sec:ideas>

the list, with a box per idea; sub-bullets are the thinking so far and what has actually been done. running experiments are dated.

- #todo *training alphazero* (currently agreement is really high, doesn't seem to yield many gains)
  - the loop exists (`train.algo: alphazero`, `configs/experiments/az-from-ppo.yaml`): self-play through the batched search with gumbel roots, a replay buffer of (row, visit-count policy, per-seat outcome), warm-started from a PPO checkpoint, judged head to head against the network it started from (against F a strong policy is at its ceiling, so vs-F can't see the gain).
  - the one run so far (2026-10-14, a 30-minute sanity run of `az-from-ppo`'s az stage in a scratch runs dir, since deleted; the numbers survive only in that session's transcript) started from a 40-minute ppo sanity network at 57.8% vs F, not from puffer-graph2: gumbel targets at 64 sims on a quarter of moves (16 on the rest), oracle search (`dets: 0`), pure self-play, lr 5e-5, 25 iterations. the raw policy fell to 46.6, 24.4 and then 18.5% vs F (600 games each), and nothing was measured head to head against its start. the bug hunt found one small bug (the gumbel temperature-0 finalist, fixed in cppanatron `5d9dda9`), which doesn't touch the targets. the policy wasn't better than its own search either: at e0 the 64-sim search scored 63.5% vs F (n=100) against the raw 57.8%. what it did find is noisy targets: with rescaled completed-q a near-single-visit q gap swings a target by ~6.5 logits, two seeds agree on the top move 87% of the time (60% on discards), knight-before-roll went from 0.90 to 0.27, and fitting all that took the network's entropy from 0.42 to 0.82 until its argmax drifted to end-turn. whether ≥ 1024-sim targets fix it is untested: v1 never trained above 512 sims, and 512 didn't move an 87% network (v1's frozen-teacher distillation of an 8 × 64-sim search, EX-1d, gave +1.3 vs F and 50.6% head to head, both null); the v2 search edge they would distill is now measured (53.4 at 1024, 55.2 at 4096, above).
  - *design (2026-10-21, CPU measurements, not run on the GPU yet):* does the search reuse above make self-play cheaper? barely. the tree survives 43% (2p) to 53% (4p) of self-play moves, but `advance` resets the budget, so today reuse saves no leaves at all; an engine top-up budget (search only `sims − kept visits`) would save 15–20% of them, and kept visits are worth about their count, so reuse can't halve the budget. transpositions buy nothing here and fight reuse (they restart the new root's shared children), so they stay off. the real lever is an eval cache across one self-play step: 31–46% fewer forwards (hashing has to move into the engine). a 1024-sim move costs ~1,000 leaf rows and ~80 ms of engine cpu; the first run as a config-only change of the existing trainer (1024-sim gumbel targets on every move, 512 games, from `puffer-graph2/public`) is ~13–22 gpu hours for 60 iterations. caveats from the critique: the 2p probes ran the engine's default rules (10 vp, discard 7) instead of base-2p's (15, 9), so the 2p numbers need a rerun; the warm-start critic is a discounted v-trace critic while az trains undiscounted win/loss, so the value head drifts during the test; and an offline distillation gate (one fixed set of 1024-sim targets, several policy-only fits judged head to head) would answer "is there anything to distill" more cheaply than the online loop. waiting on which network and which first step.
  - *ran 2026-10-21 15:40–18:53, stopped at e8* (on wandb: #wb("az-2p-2048-main-7e569a67")[az-2p-2048/main]): marco's pick, the 2p specialist straight into the self-play loop at 2048-sim targets, engine work only after a positive result. `configs/experiments/az-2p-2048.yaml`: from `puffer-graph2/public@e3600` (actor and critic), base-2p, 512 games in flight, fair gumbel search (`dets: 1`, m = 16) on every move, 30 iterations × 16,384 targets, lr 5e-5; a head to head against the start every 2 iterations (4,000 games), raw finals on `heldout-2p` (vs the start, vs F, and best and start each vs the belief-averaged `puffer-graph2/full`, paired by board), the search finals only if the raw result is positive. new logging-only monitors (bit-identical training with and without them): the value head on finished games before it trains on them, how often the search overrules the network's argmax, and drift from the start on a fixed probe of 4,096 positions. the cpu probes under the right rules first: 2048-sim targets cut the prior's distance to an 8192-sim reference from 0.295 to 0.197 tv (the reference's own noise floor is 0.179) and change the top move at 20% of positions, but they're barely better than 1024 (−0.016 #ci[−0.034][0.001]); robber moves and positions where the opponent holds a hidden vp card stay noisy. first iteration: 23.6k leaves/s, 24 min, so ~12 h for the 30.
  - *result: stopped at e8* (3.2 h) by the review's rule (two heads to head ≤ 48.5 in a row). the raw policy lost to its start: *48.0* at e6 and *48.2* at e8 (4,000 games each; the first update was in iteration 5, once enough games had finished), so the best stayed e0 and there are no finals to play. it went the way the 2026-10-14 run went, only slower: the network's entropy climbed (0.088 → 0.125), the search overruled its argmax more often (0.18 → 0.23, where a policy that learns from the search should make that rarer) and knight-before-roll fell from 0.53 to 0.43 on the drift probe. the critic wasn't it. the monitor's out-of-sample value loss rose from 0.20 to 0.54, but that's the games getting longer (the first games to finish are the short ones): the starting network on the same rows scores 0.375 / 0.508 / 0.387 at iterations 6–8, against the trained net's 0.359 / 0.538 / 0.402, so the critic moved by a few hundredths either way. the targets were it, as far as one look can say. gumbel's σ(q) = (50 + max visits) · 0.1 · q, with q min-max rescaled to [0, 1] across the root's moves, puts a ~100-logit spread between the best and the worst move at 2048 simulations, whatever the gap in win probability between them. so every one of the 16,153 two-move decisions in the replay (knight or roll, mostly) got a target of ≥ 0.99 on one move, and so did 81% of the decisions with more than eight legal moves. on the knight-or-roll decisions the start was ≥ 90% sure 98% of the time, the targets went against its argmax 21% of the time, and they play the knight 44% of the time against the start's 53%: the drift the network then learned. the moves behind those targets are good ones: `gumbel-edge-2p` (18:56–22:50, #wb("gumbel-edge-2p-main-efda50ac")[gumbel-edge-2p/main]), the same 2048-sim gumbel search with the start's weights against its raw policy, wins *62.4* #ci[59.4][65.3] of 1,000 games (`831eea84`), far above puct over the same policy (54.9 at 1024 sims, 55.2 at 4096). so the search had plenty to teach and the raw policy still got worse learning it; softer targets (no rescale, or a smaller scale) need the two constants exposed to the trainer. marco's point on the stop: the raw policy is the wrong thing to judge alphazero by, since the goal is a better network inside the search. the sound test is the trained network in the same search against the same raw start, paired game by game with the row above (mcnemar over the shared game ids, `tools/search_probe.py --pair`): `gumbel-edge-2p-e8` (22:51–02:59), e8 in the same 2048-sim gumbel search against the same raw start: *61.8* (`c4ea7b52`) against the start's weights' 62.4, paired over the 1,000 shared games *−0.6 pp #ci[−3.7][2.5]* (124 games won only by e8, 130 only by e0, mcnemar p = 0.75). only a quarter of the pairs differ at all (the board, the seat and the dice decide most games), so the pairing roughly halves the interval that two independent rows would give. the training didn't make a better search network either: the raw policy lost two points and the search played the same, so the stop didn't cost anything at e8, but by luck; the next run is judged in the harness.
  - *running since 2026-10-22 15:49* (live on wandb: #wb("az-2p-2048-soft-main-8105fbb1")[az-2p-2048-soft/main]): marco's next step, softer targets with the search left alone. the trainer now rebuilds gumbel's improved policy itself from each search's visits and q and the network's outputs at the root (`train.policy_target: gumbel`; at the engine's constants it is the engine's target to ~3e-8, and the default trains bit-identically to before). the scale was picked on cpu: 600 positions of the start's raw-policy base-2p self-play, two 2048-sim searches each, scored by −log of the target's mass on an 8192-sim search's move. a gentler σ alone softens nothing: the start's prior is itself one-hot (a median gap of 47 nats between its top two logits on knight-or-roll decisions), so softmax(prior + σ) stays one-hot at any scale, and no σ-only setting scored apart from the engine's (the best −0.10 #ci[−0.24][0.03] nats). what does soften it is tempering the prior inside the target: softmax(logits / 10 + (50 + max visits) · 0.15 · q), q unrescaled (my proposal from the probe, approved by marco). it keeps the same first choice (agrees with the 8192 move 80.5% of the time against 81.4%) and spreads the rest where the deep search is unsure: −1.12 #ci[−1.39][−0.86] nats on the score, entropy 0.64 against 0.05, ≥ 0.99 on one move in 22% of decisions against 87%. that is calibration, not better moves: two 8192-sim searches pick the same move only 86% of the time, and the expected regret under the softer target is slightly worse (+0.05 #ci[0.01][0.08] win points). whether a network trained on it searches better is what the run tests; everything else is `az-2p-2048`'s (`configs/experiments/az-2p-2048-soft.yaml`). it is judged in the search harness throughout, as marco asked: every 5th iteration the checkpoint in a 512-sim gumbel search plays the raw start on the same 1,000 select games (e0's weights are played too, so each select pairs with e0's), and the finals put the best and the last checkpoint in the 2048-sim search against the raw start on `gumbel-edge-2p`'s games, each paired with `831eea84` by mcnemar (holm over the two). every iteration's weights are kept, so an e8 row can pair with `c4ea7b52` later. ~19 h of training and selects, then 4–8 h of finals. stop rule: only a collapse, two selects in a row more than 4 pp below e0's on the same games (about 2.5 paired sds); the raw policy is not a stop criterion this time. selects so far (512 sims, paired with e0 over the same 1,000 games): e0 57.4, e5 56.0 (−1.4 pp, 129 games won only by e0 against 115 only by e5, mcnemar p = 0.41), e10 53.8 (−3.6 pp, 158 against 122, p = 0.036), so the search network is getting worse, not better, though the rule hasn't tripped. the monitors say why: by e13 the network's entropy is 0.68 (0.09 at the start) and it agrees with the start's argmax on 72% of the drift probe, and the search overrules its argmax 41% of the time (18% at the start). the softer target is pulling the prior flat, as the fixed-point note predicted, faster than the search's q can replace what the prior knew.
