  - first step is the LSTM arm of the arch ablation (a recurrent state over the seat's own row history is the cheap end of this scope); a transformer over the whole history is the expensive end and only makes sense if the LSTM beats the markov graph net at `private` level. the lstm class exists since 2026-10-17 (section 1); its 100M-step cell is in the ablation queue.
  - *2026-10-18, the lstm arm:* it ties the markov graph net head to head, 51.0 #ci[49.7][52.4] on 5,000 games, and against F (90.3 vs 90.7), at `private` level where there is hidden information to remember (@tab:ablation). so at 100M steps in 2p, memory over the seat's own row history buys nothing measurable: the row already carries played cards, hand sizes and the robber history the net needs. the transformer end of this scope is parked unless the lstm shows a gap at the full budget or in 4p, where more is hidden; the cheap next check is the lstm at 4p against `ppo-4p`'s recipe.
  - *2026-10-19, the lstm at 4p: memory buys four points head to head.* `configs/experiments/lstm-4p.yaml` ran 14:18–16:58 (2.53 h of training and selects at 22.3k steps/s, 27% slower than `graph-s`'s 30.5k; peak 5.2 GiB): the `lstm` preset on `ppo-4p`'s recipe at `aux-hidden-4p`'s budget (750 epochs, 49.2M decisions, the lambda critic), paired against `aux-hidden-4p/plain@best`, the markov `graph-s` cell of the same recipe, budget, seed and critic. against three F: *65.5* #ci[64.0][67.0] (`9198cef1`) against the plain's 64.0 #ci[62.5][65.5], 1.5 points, inside the noise; and the two select curves sit within one half-width of each other at all fifteen points (the lstm's best is its last, e750 at 65.2, and it was still climbing: 62.3, 61.8, 64.4, 65.2 from e600, where the plain's has been flat since e550). head to head at one table with two F in both slot orders (`5efc75ba`, `cf8368f4`, 8,000 games): *45.2 against 40.8*, a paired difference of +4.4 pp [2.4, 6.3]. the slot after the F costs about 10 points in both orders (38.0 against 48.0 by slot, the tracker item's artifact for the fourth time); the lstm pays it in one order (39.7 against 45.3) and the plain in the other (36.3 against 50.7), and the average over the two is the paired reading. so what bought nothing in 2p (51.0) buys four points at 4p against the same net without memory, while the F,F,F row and the select curve do not separate them: the gain shows against the markov net, not against F. one seed. the lambda critic calibrates on the recurrent net as on the plain one (seat sum 1.59 at e21, on 1.00 by e250, mean 1.000 over e300–750). the transformer end of this scope is no longer parked by the data; what the memory is reading is the next question, below.
  - *2026-10-19, marco's test: does the memory read hands? no.* the lstm's expected contribution at 4p is inferring the other hands from activity: what a seat built, what it chose not to build, who stole from whom, which the row's tracker (a card counter) cannot encode. the gnn + lstm would do this in its internal representation, if at all, so test it explicitly with the aux item's hidden-feature head: `configs/experiments/lstm-aux-4p.yaml` ran 17:00–19:41 (2.58 h at 21.8k steps/s, peak 5.4 GiB), `lstm-4p` plus the head (coefficient 0.3, the `full` players block as the target) on the memory-injected embeddings, so its per-feature R² against `aux-hidden-4p/aux`'s markov head says how much hand information the memory adds beyond the tracker row. the answer is none: over the last 50 epochs the resources are 0.47–0.55 with memory against 0.48–0.59 without (wood 0.47 vs 0.55, wheat 0.55 vs 0.59, ore 0.52 vs 0.57, brick and sheep equal), the actual vp count 0.95 against 0.95, vp cards 0.63 against 0.65, knights 0.51 against 0.51, the playable knight 0.42 against 0.42; the only features the memory lifts are road building, 0.26 against 0.20 (playable 0.24 against 0.19), and the head's own loss is higher with memory (0.020 against 0.017). the same picture at matched epochs 100, 300 and 500, so it is not a matter of training time. the policy: *66.2* #ci[64.7][67.6] against three F (`722b7168`; `lstm-4p`'s 65.5), select best e750 at 68.4 (the best-of-sixteen optimism again); head to head against `lstm-4p/main@best` at one table with two F in both slot orders (`561c7ee2`, `f691b7fc`, 8,000 games) 43.8 against 42.3, a paired +1.5 pp [−0.4, 3.5]: the aux-shaped memory does not move the policy either, as the head did not move the markov policy. so the four points the memory bought against the markov net (above) are not read off the hands: whatever the recurrent state carries, the hand regression cannot extract more of the other seats' cards from it than from the tracker row. one coefficient (0.3, not re-measured for the lstm); the recurrent aux path needed building for this (`tests/test_lstm_aux.py`, config schema 4). what the memory does carry is open: the order of events, or who did what to whom, are the candidates the hand features do not cover.
